The js row in the engine table: $(js chunk[,input]) runs the chunk, JavaScript source
text, whole with its input on stdin; what it prints is the result, and an uncaught error
prints its message and stack on stderr. The row's name gives the --js flag; there is no
multi-call name and no argv companion.

QuickJS is a library plus a cli, and amk wants the library: the guest build skips qjs.c,
qjsc.c and the two generator programs, and compiles guest/js_main.c in their place, which
is where js_run_main lives. That entry point sets the engine up the way the qjs cli does,
with the std and os modules bound as globals so a plain chunk reaches std.in and os.exec
without an import; a chunk that is a module is detected and run as one, and a script
chunk runs with the async flag so it may await at top level. QuickJS ships a Makefile
that only builds its programs, so its guest rule compiles the sources directly instead
of running one.

--- a/src/amk.h
+++ b/src/amk.h
@@ -23,5 +23,6 @@
 extern int s7_run_main (int argc, char **argv);
 extern int micropy_run_main (int argc, char **argv);
 extern int wasm3_main (int argc, char **argv);
+extern int js_run_main (int argc, char **argv);
 
 #endif
--- a/src/function.c
+++ b/src/function.c
@@ -2511,6 +2511,9 @@
 #ifdef AMK_ENGINE_WASM3
     { "wasm", "wasm3", wasm3_main, 0, 1, 1 },
 #endif
+#ifdef AMK_ENGINE_QUICKJS
+    { "js", 0, js_run_main, 0, 0, 0 },
+#endif
     { 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 }
   };
 
